# OPS payments demo - start page
payments.start.title = Pay for your disclosure
payments.start.heading = Pay for your disclosure
payments.start.intro = This is the amount due for your disclosure. When you continue, the service raises the charge and takes you to the HMRC payment service to choose how to pay. You do not need to enter an amount or a reference.
payments.start.disclosureId = Disclosure
payments.start.tax = Tax owed
payments.start.interest = Interest
payments.start.penalty = Penalty
payments.start.total = Total to pay
payments.start.submit = Continue to payment
payments.start.details.summary = Technical details (for demonstration)
payments.start.details.explanation = When you continue, the service raises a charge on the corporate tier (stubbed) to get a charge reference, then calls pay-api to start a payment journey (SPJ) — passing the charge reference, the amount and the return URL. pay-api returns a journey id and a nextUrl, and the service records the journey before redirecting your browser to nextUrl on pay-frontend, where you choose a payment method. The card or bank details are never handled by this service.

# OPS payments demo - return page
payments.return.title = Payment result
payments.return.success.heading = Payment received
payments.return.success.chargeRef = Charge reference {0}
payments.return.failed.heading = Payment failed
payments.return.failed.body = The payment was not successful. The disclosure has not been recorded as paid. You can try again.
payments.return.cancelled.heading = Payment cancelled
payments.return.cancelled.body = You cancelled the payment. The disclosure has not been recorded as paid. You can try again.
payments.return.pending.heading = Payment not yet confirmed
payments.return.pending.body = We could not confirm the payment with pay-api. The disclosure has not been recorded as paid. The browser redirect on its own is not proof of payment, so the service only records a disclosure as paid once pay-api confirms it.
payments.return.unknown.heading = Payment journey not found
payments.return.unknown.body = We could not find a record of this payment journey. It may have expired.
payments.return.disclosureId = Disclosure
payments.return.journeyId = Payment journey id
payments.return.status = pay-api status
payments.return.chargeRef = Charge reference
payments.return.notification.heading = Recording the payment
payments.return.notification.body = After confirming the payment succeeded, the service sent a charge-reference notification to the corporate tier so ETMP can record settlement and a caseworker can see the disclosure is paid. In production this is either sent automatically by OPS or routed by the service to ETMP via HIP.
payments.return.notification.outcome = Notification result
payments.return.startAgain = Start another payment

# NRS evidencing demo - hub
nrs.demo.title = NRS proof of concept
nrs.demo.heading = NRS proof of concept
nrs.demo.intro = This demonstrates how DDS would record a legally meaningful disclosure submission in HMRC''s Non-Repudiation Store (NRS) — an immutable, tamper-evident evidence trail.
nrs.demo.button = Try NRS submission
nrs.demo.howItWorks.heading = How NRS evidencing works
nrs.demo.howItWorks.step1 = The user confirms a declaration and submits a disclosure snapshot.
nrs.demo.howItWorks.step2 = The service builds a JSON evidence payload, computes a SHA-256 checksum, and base64-encodes the payload.
nrs.demo.howItWorks.step3 = It POSTs the platform NRS request (payload + metadata including identity, search keys, and receipt/declaration).
nrs.demo.howItWorks.step4 = NRS validates the checksum and returns 202 Accepted with an nrSubmissionId (or 419 / 5xx on failure).
nrs.demo.howItWorks.step5 = The service persists journey state and shows confirmation, queued, or failure messaging — including retry with the same nrSubmissionId.
nrs.demo.open.heading = Still open (not decided by this PoC)
nrs.demo.open.h11 = H11 — What event is legally binding for non-repudiation?
nrs.demo.open.h12 = H12 — Evidence retention and retrieval requirements?
nrs.demo.open.lb6 = LB6 — Sync (user waits) versus fire-and-confirm?
nrs.demo.open.lb11 = LB11 — Are Upscan document references included in the hash?

# NRS evidencing demo - start
nrs.start.title = Submit disclosure evidence
nrs.start.heading = Submit disclosure evidence
nrs.start.intro = Enter a stub disclosure, confirm the declaration, then optionally simulate an NRS failure. When you continue, the service builds an NRS evidence request and calls the (stubbed) Non-Repudiation Store.
nrs.start.error.heading = There is a problem
nrs.start.fullName.label = Full name
nrs.start.regime.label = Tax regime
nrs.start.amount.label = Amount (pence)
nrs.start.amount.hint = Enter the amount in pence, for example 150000 for £1,500.00
nrs.start.declaration.legend = Declaration
nrs.start.declaration.label = I agree — the information is correct and complete
nrs.start.declaration.error = You must agree to the declaration to submit
nrs.start.simulate.label = Simulate NRS outcome (demo only)
nrs.start.simulate.hint = Use this to exercise failure and retry paths without a real NRS environment.
nrs.start.simulate.none = Success (202 Accepted)
nrs.start.simulate.unavailable = NRS unavailable (503)
nrs.start.simulate.checksum = Checksum failure (419)
nrs.start.details.summary = Technical details (for demonstration)
nrs.start.details.explanation = The service builds DisclosureEvidence JSON, SHA-256 hashes the UTF-8 bytes, base64-encodes the payload, and POSTs to NRS with businessId/notableEvent placeholders (dds / dds-disclosure-submission). Locally this hits an in-process stub that validates the checksum and returns nrSubmissionId. Production would call the platform non-repudiation service after Legal confirms the binding event.
nrs.start.submit = Submit to NRS

